WEBVTT

00:00.350 --> 00:03.105
One dollar today, or one
dollar in ten years?

00:03.745 --> 00:04.686
Same dollar…

00:05.226 --> 00:07.810
and yet, to an actuary,
not the same value.

00:08.170 --> 00:08.834
Why?

00:09.474 --> 00:13.871
In a few minutes, we'll see why time has
a price, and how an actuary works it out.

00:14.761 --> 00:17.494
Take JG's Holding, our fictional insurer.

00:17.854 --> 00:21.398
It collects premiums today and
pays out benefits years later.

00:21.758 --> 00:24.790
In between, the money is
invested and earns interest.

00:25.680 --> 00:29.693
Let's invest one thousand dollars
at two point five percent a year.

00:30.053 --> 00:33.448
After one year, we have one
thousand and twenty-five dollars.

00:33.808 --> 00:37.757
After two years, one thousand and
fifty dollars and sixty-three cents:

00:37.917 --> 00:39.925
the interest has itself earned interest.

00:40.285 --> 00:43.893
After ten years, about one thousand
two hundred and eighty dollars.

00:44.783 --> 00:49.244
Multiplying by one plus i, n times,
moves money forward in time:

00:49.404 --> 00:50.708
that's accumulation.

00:51.068 --> 00:52.479
Dividing moves it back:

00:52.639 --> 00:53.794
that's discounting.

00:54.154 --> 00:57.676
Actuaries write v for one
divided by one plus i.

00:58.036 --> 01:02.360
So one thousand dollars promised
in ten years is worth one thousand

01:02.400 --> 01:06.724
divided by one point zero two five
to the power of ten today, about

01:06.764 --> 01:10.242
seven hundred and eighty-one
dollars and twenty cents.

01:11.132 --> 01:14.057
The further away the payment,
the less it's worth today.

01:14.417 --> 01:18.943
In ten years, one thousand dollars is
worth about seven hundred and eighty-one.

01:19.303 --> 01:21.396
In twenty years, six hundred and ten.

01:21.756 --> 01:24.767
In thirty years, only four
hundred and seventy-seven.

01:25.657 --> 01:28.839
And what if JG's Holding has
to pay one thousand dollars

01:28.879 --> 01:31.313
at the end of each of
the next three years?

01:31.953 --> 01:33.406
We discount each payment.

01:33.766 --> 01:38.142
The first is worth nine hundred and
seventy-five dollars and sixty-one cents.

01:38.502 --> 01:42.494
The second, nine hundred and fifty-one
dollars and eighty-one cents.

01:42.854 --> 01:46.441
The third, nine hundred and
twenty-eight dollars and sixty cents.

01:46.801 --> 01:49.644
In total, two thousand
eight hundred and fifty-six

01:49.684 --> 01:51.604
dollars today, not three thousand:

01:51.764 --> 01:52.876
that's an annuity.

01:53.766 --> 01:56.222
Your turn, and pause the
video if you need to.

01:56.582 --> 02:00.254
JG's Holding must pay five
thousand dollars in five years.

02:00.614 --> 02:02.793
The rate is two point five percent a year.

02:03.153 --> 02:04.798
How much should it set aside today?

02:18.188 --> 02:19.855
We discount over five years.

02:20.215 --> 02:25.636
One point zero two five to the power of
five is about one point one three one four.

02:25.996 --> 02:29.636
Five thousand divided by that
gives about four thousand four

02:29.676 --> 02:32.825
hundred and nineteen dollars
and twenty-seven cents.

02:33.715 --> 02:34.592
Key points.

02:34.952 --> 02:39.200
To move forward in time, multiply
by one plus i, to the power n.

02:39.560 --> 02:42.955
To come back to today,
multiply by v to the power n.

02:43.315 --> 02:46.262
And for several payments,
add up their present values.

02:47.152 --> 02:48.243
Next episode:

02:48.403 --> 02:51.136
is one percent a month really
twelve percent a year?

02:51.776 --> 02:54.332
If this video helped,
subscribe, leave a like and

02:54.372 --> 02:56.664
share it with someone
who'll find it useful.

02:57.024 --> 02:58.008
Got a question?

02:58.648 --> 02:59.803
Ask in the comments.

03:00.163 --> 03:01.403
Thanks for watching!

03:02.293 --> 03:03.320
ACTU-BASICS.

03:03.680 --> 03:05.133
Enlightening through numbers.
